WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.721
A memory leak occurs when allocated memory
remains reserved but the program can no

2
00:00:04.801 --> 00:00:06.330
longer release it correctly.

3
00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:10.264
In C, this most often means dynamic memory
was allocated and the last usable

4
00:00:10.344 --> 00:00:14.014
ownership path to that allocation was
lost, skipped, or never defined.

5
00:00:14.094 --> 00:00:16.235
The memory may still exist in the process,

6
00:00:16.315 --> 00:00:18.762
but the program has no reliable way to
return it.

7
00:00:18.842 --> 00:00:20.921
Leaks are not always immediate crashes.

8
00:00:21.001 --> 00:00:23.693
A small allocation missed once may be hard
to notice.

9
00:00:23.773 --> 00:00:27.321
The same missed cleanup repeated in a
loop, request handler, parser,

10
00:00:27.401 --> 00:00:28.808
batch job, service thread,

11
00:00:28.888 --> 00:00:32.680
or library call can gradually consume
memory until reliability suffers.

12
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.186
That slow accumulation is what makes leaks
easy to underestimate.

13
00:00:36.266 --> 00:00:40.119
Lost heap memory can reduce reliability,
availability, and performance.

14
00:00:40.199 --> 00:00:44.175
A process with growing memory use can slow
down, trigger allocator pressure,

15
00:00:44.255 --> 00:00:46.213
force restarts, fail under workload,

16
00:00:46.293 --> 00:00:48.984
or interfere with other software on the
same system.

17
00:00:49.064 --> 00:00:52.673
In constrained environments, even small
recurring leaks can matter.

18
00:00:52.753 --> 00:00:56.546
Long-running programs, embedded systems,
daemons, services, plugins,

19
00:00:56.626 --> 00:01:00.724
and libraries are especially sensitive to
leaks because they may not get a clean

20
00:01:00.804 --> 00:01:02.884
process restart after each unit of work.

21
00:01:02.964 --> 00:01:06.512
A command-line tool that exits immediately
may hide sloppy cleanup,

22
00:01:06.592 --> 00:01:10.507
while a service that runs for months
cannot rely on process exit as its normal

23
00:01:10.587 --> 00:01:11.565
cleanup strategy.

24
00:01:11.645 --> 00:01:15.560
Leak prevention starts by treating
ownership and cleanup as part of design,

25
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:16.864
not as an afterthought.

26
00:01:16.944 --> 00:01:20.614
Every allocation should have an intended
owner, an expected lifetime,

27
00:01:20.694 --> 00:01:21.672
and a release path.

28
00:01:21.752 --> 00:01:25.178
The safer habit is to make cleanup visible
when the code is written,

29
00:01:25.258 --> 00:01:28.500
rather than hoping a later review finds
every missing release.
